            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary meaning of wings is divine protection and refuge, typically drawing on the imagery of a bird sheltering its young. A well-recognized secondary meaning relates to the underlying Hebrew word (kanaph) also meaning the edge or corner of a garment, which symbolizes covenant protection, marriage, and the law (via tassels).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The symbol represents the 'covenant edge'—broadly encompassing wings, skirts, borders, and corners—serving as both a place of refuge and a reminder of the law.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Avian wings symbolizing divine protection, sheltering, and refuge.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The covenant edge (including wings and garment borders) representing both a place of refuge and the binding of the law.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus primarily restricts the core symbol to avian imagery of protection, treating the garment edge as a secondary, distinct alternative based on Hebrew polysemy. The book elevates and broadens the definition, uniting both the avian 'refuge' and vestiary 'law/covenant' motifs into a single overarching concept of the 'covenant edge.'\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book does not reject the consensus view of refuge but rather merges it with the alternative consensus view (garment edge/covenant/law) to form a unified, broader definition.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By defining the symbol fundamentally as the 'covenant edge,' the book takes the primary consensus meaning (refuge) and a major alternative meaning (garment borders/law) and integrates them. This shifts the central referent from purely avian wings to a broader conceptual category of edges and borders.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"By conceptualizing wings fundamentally as a 'covenant edge,' the book somewhat overshadows the natural, universal imagery of a mother bird protecting her young, which is the immediate and most prominent context for biblical passages describing refuge under God's wings (e.g., Psalm 91:4, Matthew 23:37).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
